From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 11:16:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 050/184] segacd: say why a read failed, and seek again when
 the drive lands late

The console reported a failure with no way to tell which of four it was,
which is the same class of mistake that cost stage 2 discs: an
instrument that says something went wrong but not what.

Now the report carries the header the decoder is actually delivering
beside the one being waited for, per-slot fail codes and sector counts,
the header attached to the data, and how many re-seeks it took. When a
read times out it takes whichever sector the decoder last wrote and
reports its header, so a timeout yields evidence instead of silence.

Behaviour changes, both grounded in the BIOS rather than guessed:

- Twenty frames of lead-in instead of two. Sega uses two because a
  seek in an emulator lands exactly; a real one lands approximately and
  then reads forward, and landing two frames late puts the sector
  behind the head for ever.
- Re-seek when the arriving headers pass the target, which is what
  $4124 does. Guarded by a settle delay, because re-seeking does not
  stop the decoder and the sectors still arriving are from where the
  head just was -- without the guard all four retries are spent in four
  frames before the drive has moved.

Holding B at power-on selects the reset-time CDC control pair alone,
so the two configurations the BIOS writes can be told apart in one
burn rather than two. The report block's magic word is now written
last and cleared first: the servant reads it by halting us, and a
half-written report reads as a coherent one.
